Close to Background for Debian/Ubuntu
====================================

This package contains the native tray helper and a matching Thunderbird add-on.
After installing the package:

1. Install and enable GNOME AppIndicator support (or another compatible tray).
2. Start or restart Thunderbird. It installs the bundled XPI automatically.
3. Open the add-on preferences. "Your tray is ready" confirms the connection.
4. Run thunderbird-closetobackground-doctor if setup is incomplete.

Use native Thunderbird 128-153, not Flatpak or Snap. GNOME Wayland is supported.
Wayland desktop/compositor minimize shortcuts cannot be observed by the add-on;
use Thunderbird's own minimize button, close button, or Send to tray instead.

Migrating from the tarball
-------------------------
An existing per-user native manifest overrides this system installation.
Disable the add-on, run ./uninstall.sh from the old extracted tarball as your
normal user, install/update this package, restart Thunderbird, then re-enable
the add-on if you previously disabled it.
The diagnostic command reports any user override, including broken ones.
The Debian installer deliberately never deletes files from user home directories.

Updates and removal
-------------------
After a package upgrade, restart Thunderbird. The local-file extension policy
installs newer bundled XPI versions automatically and preserves disabled state.
Use apt remove to remove the helper and restore the policy. Restart Thunderbird
to release policy management, then remove the remaining profile copy in Add-ons
and Themes if desired. Package scripts never edit or delete user profiles.
Without a helper the add-on leaves mail windows visible.

Automatic installation
----------------------
The package uses ExtensionSettings/normal_installed with a local file URL.
The add-on can be disabled, but cannot be removed while the policy is active.
Updates come from this package rather than a separate add-on update server.
This applies to profiles using the installation's policies. Installing the
package authorizes the add-on's unrestricted-access Experiment API; there is
no separate interactive XPI permission prompt. No network download is needed.

The maintainer scripts merge only this add-on's entry into
/etc/thunderbird/policies/policies.json, or an existing
/usr/lib/thunderbird/distribution/policies.json when no system policy exists.
They preserve other entries and restore the original bytes if no other changes
were made. Later administrator edits are preserved. Recovery information is
stored privately in /var/lib/thunderbird-closetobackground/policy-state.json.
Invalid policies and conflicting policies for this add-on stop configuration
with an error instead of being overwritten. After resolving a conflict, run
sudo dpkg-reconfigure thunderbird-closetobackground.
There are no services or autostart entries, and GNOME settings are unchanged.

See /usr/share/doc/thunderbird-closetobackground/README.md and docs/packaging.md
for installation, reproducible builds, checksums, and release instructions.
